In our lightweight work frame where HMM feature is absent, the sparse node feature representation becomes even scarcer. Furthermore, the shrinkage on nrdb for PSSM encoding poses challenges in accurately representing amino acid features. Therefore, we utilized multiple pre-trained protein language models to obtain informative amino acid feature embeddings (Figure 1C) [36, 37]. First, the pretrained SeqVec model (‘sv’ in the following description) is adapted [36]. By training EMLo on the UniRef50 dataset, SeqVec can generate continuous embeddings at amino acid level. In this step, the peptide sequences are fed into the pre-trained SeqVec model to obtain amino acid-level embeddings for each sequence. As a result, the model outputs a vector with 1024 dimensions for each amino acid. Due to the varying lengths of sequences, we extract embeddings according to the maximum sequence length (\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
